<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Becket</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1119–<a href="/years/1170">1170</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Saint Thomas of Canterbury, Thomas of London, Thomas à Becket</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Becket was an English cleric and statesman who served as Lord Chancellor from 1155 to 1162 and as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 1162 until his murder in 1170. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to a Norman family, he rose in the household of Archbishop Theobald of Bec, who recommended him to Henry II. As archbishop he resisted the king's attempt to reassert royal prerogatives over the Church; convicted in 1164 of contempt of royal authority, he spent six years in exile under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>. Soon after his return in 1170 four knights loyal to Henry killed him in Canterbury Cathedral. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> canonized him in 1173, and he is venerated as a saint and martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-becket.jpg" alt="Thomas Becket" width="238" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Miniaturist, English (active 1170s in Cirenchester) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London._British_Library,_Cotton_MS_Claudius_B_II._f.341r_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Learning and royal service could carry a man of modest birth to these offices: <b>Thomas Becket</b>, a London merchant’s son, became Archbishop of Canterbury in 1162.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>It came after Henry's agents destroyed the shrine of <b>Thomas Becket</b> at Canterbury.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Four knights, believing they were carrying out Henry II's wishes, killed Archbishop <b>Thomas Becket</b> in his cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
